why 2 0 CROSSING The tenor drops to F♯ under the bass's A♯ for one beat, then climbs G♯–A♯. The bass's neighbor motion and the tenor's dip cross briefly.
why 2 0 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and tenor move from F♯/B to C♯/F♯ in contrary motion, the soprano leaping up and the tenor down. Bach accepts contrary fifths; strict exercises avoid them.
why 4 0 LEADING_TONE The tenor's A♯, leading tone of B, leaps up to D♯, the third of the B chord at the fermata. Rising to B would leave the chord without its third.
why 6 1/2 DIRECT The tune leaps down F♯–C♯ while the bass steps G♯–F♯: a hidden fifth softened by the step in the bass. The leap belongs to the hymn tune.
why 7 0 SIX_FOUR This is a cadential ⁶₄ in C♯: the chord over G♯ moves to G♯ major and then to C♯ at the fermata.
why 10 1/4 LEAPS After its leap down to D♯ the bass keeps falling to B before turning up to E, outlining the B chord on the way.
why 13 1/4 CROSSING The bass leaps up an octave to C♯, above the tenor's A, and steps down to B: a short crossing caused by the octave leap.
why 13 1/4 RANGE The bass's octave leap takes it up to C♯ for a beat before it steps down again.
why 15 1/4 OVERLAP The tune leaps up to E and the alto leaps up to B with it, above where the soprano was. They move together, so the order stays clear.
